From 6d2b0363a5bf02d26bd89053d6c145d89eb56eba Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ash Pera <ashley.pera@noaa.gov>
Date: Wed, 14 Oct 2026 19:55:06 -0600
Subject: [PATCH 1/3] TEMPO: close the water budget (settling, graupel
 re-closure, sedimentation density, clips)

Ports of four scheme-internal fixes measured and tested in the CCPP copy of
Thompson in UFS (ufs-community/ccpp-physics fixes C1, C2, C3, M1; C96
control_p8_ugwpv1 with in-model water meters). There, together with the
driver fixes, they reduce the microphysics' loss of about 0.3 % of
grid-scale precipitation to about 2e-8 of it; closing to rounding also needs
M5, the third commit of this series. TEMPO keeps the same logic in
restructured form:

C1  Cloud water settling out of the lowest layer (cloud_precip) entered only
    the freezing-rain diagnostic. Add it to rain_precip after that
    diagnostic and before the frozen fraction, so that the fraction's total
    includes it. Cloud sedimentation also took one full time step where
    rain, ice, snow and graupel substep, so where vt*dt > dz a layer lost
    more cloud than it held and the clips filled the overdraft from vapour.
    Substep it with the same Courant rule (at every level with a nonzero
    fall speed); with one substep the arithmetic is unchanged.
C2  check_over_depletion rescales the rain and ice (snow) losses to rain-ice
    and, below freezing, rain-snow collection, but not the graupel gains
    prg_rci and prg_rcs, so the limiters created graupel. Recompute both
    after the limiters, with their graupel volume (this version's own volume
    expressions). Below freezing the rain limiter can shrink the rain lost
    to rain-snow collection while the snow gain stays, which would make the
    recomputed prg_rcs negative: cap the snow gain at the rain lost to it
    first.
C3  Between the tendency update and sedimentation, rho was recomputed three
    times from the temperature after latent heating, and all mass
    conversions through sedimentation used it. Keep rho at the entry
    density; pass the updated density (rhon) only to thermo_vars, for fall
    speeds and ventilation.
M1  Condensate ending the step at or below r1 was deleted by the final
    clips. Exchange it with vapour first, with lvap (cloud, rain) or lsub
    (ice, snow, graupel).
The UFS series' fifth fix of this kind (M2: count all of the lowest layer's
sedimentation as precipitation, not only above low_limit_mass_for_precip)
is held separately. The column numbers below were measured with it; without
it, the rain leaving the lowest layer below that limit is uncounted again
(not re-measured here).

And one fix of this version's own (v3.0.5 and later already have it):

F1  Standard (flux-form) rain and graupel sedimentation fixed the number of
    substeps from the fall speeds before the loop, then recomputed the fall
    speeds after every substep, so with faster fall speeds a layer could
    lose more than it held. The overdraft became negative condensate that
    the clips filled with created water: up to 3.7 kg/m2 in one call of a
    random column and 1.4e5 kg/m2 in a single graupel layer (dt = 600 s,
    hail-aware). The recomputation is commented out exactly as in v3.0.5,
    so the substeps keep every layer's outflow within its content. This
    changes behaviour with semi_sedi_flag off: the fall speeds stay those
    of the start of the step (as from v3.0.5), and snow, which takes the
    rain fall speed for its boost, sees those too. In the test column below
    the hour's precipitation changes by +2.3e-2 kg/m2 (+7.6e-4) at 20 s and
    +5.6e-3 kg/m2 (+1.8e-4) at 60 s.

Measured with TEMPO's 59-level test column (test/data/mpas_59lev_test.txt),
full microphysics, and a water-closure check of every call against the entry
dry-air density (gfortran, double precision): sum(W1 - W0 + P)/sum(P), 20 s
x 180 / 60 s x 60 steps. Standard sedimentation: 4.2e-5 / 1.1e-3 before,
2.9e-8 / 4.3e-8 after; the remaining residual is water created at up to
2.2e-8 kg/m2 per step, 4.8e-9 per step on average at 20 s and 2.2e-8 at 60
s, which the third commit removes. With semi-Lagrangian sedimentation:
4.1e-3 / 2.7e-3 before, 3.3e-3 / 2.2e-3 after; the rest is the next two
commits. (In this version tempo_init resets tempo_cfgs, so the test passes
semi_sedi_flag to tempo_init.)

The cloud substepping and the cap do not act in that column (its result is
bit for bit the same without them). Two one-call cases (dt = 600 s) do:

- fog only in a 5 m lowest layer (Courant number 5.6), microphysics off:
  without substeps the surface received 5.6 times the cloud the layer held
  (0.051 against 0.0091 kg/m2), the excess drawn from the layer's vapour;
  with the 6 substeps, 1.000 times and no vapour drawn.
- cold rain and snow, one level per combination of 258-270 K and rain and
  snow contents of 1e-5 to 1e-3 kg/kg: the rain limiter left one level's
  re-closed prg_rcs at -1.5e-8 kg m-3 s-1, 9.1e-4 kg/m2 of negative graupel
  that the intermediate checks then deleted (water created); with the cap
  the call's residual is 3.2e-9 kg/m2, the level the third commit removes.

Not changed here: the intermediate *_check_and_update calls overwrite the
tendencies of species at or below r1 mid-step, which deletes water; the
third commit sends it to vapour instead.

AI-assisted: written with Claude Code (Anthropic); reviewed by the author.
